"# }) {
        code {
            Markdown("**Needs:** nothing besides the page. It claims `/`: paste it into a new project, or replace the starter's `src/pages/Home.wf` with it.")
            CodeBlock(#"page Landing(path: "/", title: "Welcome", description: "A page that introduces itself.") {
    sequence {
        step { Heading("Welcome").h1.fadeIn }
        step(after: "120ms") { Text("What we do.").slideUp }
        step(after: "120ms") { Button("Start").primary.slideUp }
    }
}"#, title: "wf")
        }
        Markdown("The heading fades in as the page opens, the line beneath it slides up a moment later, and the button slides up last, so the page arrives in order rather than all at once.\n\n- `after:` is measured from the step before it, so the three start at 0ms, 120ms and 240ms.\n- A step with no `after:` starts when the step before it does, which is how several elements move together.\n- A `sequence` does nothing but timing: it writes the clock onto its elements as `delay:` and leaves nothing of itself behind. What the rest of the build sees is the program you would have written by hand, with `delay: \"120ms\"` and `delay: \"240ms\"` on the second and third elements.")

    }
}
